WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:04.870
- Everybody ready?

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:06.507
One, two, three..

00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:11.293
All right, somewhere about here.

00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:18.030
- We're gonna lay the balloon
and the basket that way,

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:19.880
so you can do the suit up right here.

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:25.033
Many years have lead up to the
flying that we're doing now.

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:28.430
I don't think I've ever looked forward to

00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:30.003
anything more than this.

00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:33.783
- Trent, I guess you're
taking care of fuel tanks?

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:38.370
Now I get to put all of this time and

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:41.390
all of this energy into
the ultimate adventure,

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:42.253
which is flight.

00:00:46.489 --> 00:00:47.873
You gotta let it keep those sides out.

00:00:49.342 --> 00:00:50.509
[String Music]

00:00:56.112 --> 00:00:57.622
Fantastic job.

00:00:57.622 --> 00:01:01.610
I get to have the history of
space and flying exploration,

00:01:01.610 --> 00:01:05.610
and I get to do it in a
spacesuit that I have built.

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:07.960
OK, you can hand me the pressure valves.

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:10.773
This is hard for me to
beat as a life experience.

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:15.254
Thank you, reading glad co.
We're gonna put my helmet on

00:01:15.254 --> 00:01:16.854
and pressurize, and go.

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:51.610
My name is Cameron Smith. I am

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:54.570
an Archaeology professor at
Portland State University.

00:01:54.570 --> 00:01:57.050
And for the past eight years
I've been building my own

00:01:57.050 --> 00:01:57.883
spacesuit.

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:02.520
When I was 12, my dad would
bring home eight millimeter

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:04.760
footage from the moon
landings, and he'd project

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:09.110
them on the bedroom wall.
And I would watch these alien

00:02:09.110 --> 00:02:12.223
scenes; that was a pretty
magical thing to me.

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:16.780
A lot of it was about something
new; a lot of it was about

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:20.083
exploration-seeing something
that hadn't been seen before.

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:24.060
Between the ages of 10 and
14, I wrote to all of the

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:27.280
Apollo Astronauts. And I was
trying to get into space; I

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:30.450
wanted to know how. They told
me to fly for the military,

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:34.210
but my eyes weren't perfect.
So, that was the end of that

00:02:34.210 --> 00:02:36.683
dream, when I was very young.

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:41.580
For a long time, I thought
if I were just a little

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:44.860
smarter, if I could be more resourceful,

00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:47.060
then I really did think
I could go to space.

00:02:48.947 --> 00:02:51.270
So, I thought the way that
I could get there was to

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:54.913
build a balloon and to
make my own space suit.

00:03:00.130 --> 00:03:04.830
For the human body space
is a very difficult place.

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:08.870
In the shade it can be
minus 300 Fahrenheit.

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:12.950
In the sunshine it can
be plus 300 Fahrenheit.

00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:14.113
That's hard to imagine.

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:19.290
For all these reasons, if
you're going into space,

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:22.463
you'd be well advised to wear a spacesuit.

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:28.000
A spacesuit is basically
a compression device.

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:31.490
It holds you together in
the absence of atmospheric

00:03:31.490 --> 00:03:32.600
pressure.

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:34.657
It has some properties that
are going to keep you warm,

00:03:34.657 --> 00:03:37.480
but is also doesn't keep
you too warm. It has to be

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:40.440
gas impermeable; it should
be flame proof, so it can't

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:43.230
catch fire. You're dealing
with fuels of all kinds, and

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:46.450
it also has to be non-stretch.
That is, you don't want it

00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:48.900
to be ballooning up when
the suit is pressurized.

00:03:49.977 --> 00:03:52.530
It's a fascinating challenge
to get all of those things

00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:54.853
functioning in one environment.

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:07.800
All right, first pressurization
attempt with the newly

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:09.213
reinstalled appendix.

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:12.710
All right, so this is a test, keeping the

00:04:13.790 --> 00:04:15.333
fire extinguisher armed.

00:04:17.557 --> 00:04:21.043
Two hundred holes built for a new visor.

00:04:22.430 --> 00:04:25.960
At 2 PSI, to be able to do that

00:04:27.391 --> 00:04:29.660
is astounding.

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:31.970
It sounds mind-boggling: how
do you build a spacesuit?

00:04:31.970 --> 00:04:35.460
Well, I started looking at
the history of the early

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:37.497
pressure suits in the 1930s.

00:04:40.678 --> 00:04:41.620
People were trying to fly

00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:45.680
to high altitudes in balloons,
and when I read their stories

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:49.160
and I learned exactly how
they built those suits,

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:51.990
and I learned that they
flew to 50 and 60,000

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:56.120
feet and most of them survived,
I thought surely if they

00:04:56.120 --> 00:04:59.010
could do that with pigskin
and leather and rubber,

00:04:59.010 --> 00:05:02.003
I can use today's materials
and do the same thing.

00:05:04.302 --> 00:05:05.135
- OK, here we go.

00:05:05.135 --> 00:05:06.900
- [Cameron] OK.

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:09.820
The basic spacesuit to
get you to orbit and back

00:05:09.820 --> 00:05:12.520
has been on the order of a $100,000,

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:15.300
and weighing almost 75 pounds.

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:18.910
My goal has been $1,000, 5 kilos

00:05:18.910 --> 00:05:22.630
or about 11 pounds; and
to reach 63,000 feet

00:05:22.630 --> 00:05:25.970
above the surface of the earth.

00:05:25.970 --> 00:05:28.752
At that altitude the only
thing that will keep me

00:05:28.752 --> 00:05:31.002
alive in my balloon
flights is my spacesuit.

00:05:40.660 --> 00:05:43.580
When I started all of
this, I used the cheapest

00:05:43.580 --> 00:05:46.840
and most available things
possible to prove the design.

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:49.400
So, for example, for the
neck ring for the helmet,

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:53.970
I was using a pie tin. I've
used common plumbing ball

00:05:53.970 --> 00:05:57.620
valves, motorcycle
batteries, aquarium pumps.

00:05:57.620 --> 00:06:00.520
Zip ties are tremendous.

00:06:00.520 --> 00:06:02.880
Basically I'm trying to
mount this valve on this side

00:06:03.718 --> 00:06:04.551
so it's not going to flop around.

00:06:04.551 --> 00:06:06.040
The least interesting way
to have done this would

00:06:06.040 --> 00:06:09.520
be to buy a Russian space
suit on Ebay, you can do that.

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:11.710
But that's not fun;
that's not interesting to

00:06:11.710 --> 00:06:14.030
just copy what somebody else has done.

00:06:14.030 --> 00:06:16.983
I wanted to reinvent the wheel myself.

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:26.968
So we will be able to
reach suit pressure, which

00:06:26.968 --> 00:06:28.160
will not come up high.

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:31.070
The margins for error on
this project are very slim,

00:06:31.070 --> 00:06:35.210
and you need to be willing
to work at this through

00:06:35.210 --> 00:06:37.073
failure after failure.

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:42.840
I have failed, and then
rebuilt the thing, and that

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:45.550
failed. And I rebuilt it
and that failed; sometimes

00:06:45.550 --> 00:06:47.790
I was trying to fix a
technical problem for a couple

00:06:47.790 --> 00:06:51.630
of years. But then I would
say, "I'm not gonna get

00:06:51.630 --> 00:06:53.893
"beaten by this and try again."

00:07:10.174 --> 00:07:12.177
OK, I'm going to start
going through a few of the

00:07:12.177 --> 00:07:14.040
set of checklists just to
be sure we have all the

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:16.780
items. So we're looking at

00:07:18.340 --> 00:07:19.990
crew gloves, and those are in

00:07:19.990 --> 00:07:23.313
the orange case. Five, six, seven eight.

00:07:24.620 --> 00:07:26.023
We have our pin keepers.

00:07:27.940 --> 00:07:30.370
Now these are flying instruments, so

00:07:30.370 --> 00:07:33.020
a little bit delicate; if
we could put these up high,

00:07:34.725 --> 00:07:36.300
so that they're not being crushed.

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:38.770
I have to run this like
a small space program.

00:07:38.770 --> 00:07:43.630
And I have to have every
checklist item theoretically

00:07:43.630 --> 00:07:48.160
known, practically understood,
practiced, simulated

00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:51.370
many times, and then I have
to fulfill that sequence

00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:54.260
perfectly during the flight.
And I know that somewhere

00:07:54.260 --> 00:07:58.340
deep in the labyrinth of the
system some little mistake

00:07:58.340 --> 00:08:02.030
could kill me. That's a
great challenge; if I wanted

00:08:02.030 --> 00:08:06.360
to do something easy I
would pick something easy.

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:07.810
But that doesn't interest me.

00:08:10.130 --> 00:08:15.130
The difficulty intrigues
me and is a source of

00:08:15.290 --> 00:08:19.530
vitality for my life.
This is all the paperwork

00:08:19.530 --> 00:08:22.580
required to fly a balloon.
Somebody asked me one

00:08:22.580 --> 00:08:25.100
time if this was a suicide
mission. I thought that

00:08:25.100 --> 00:08:28.570
was rather stupid. If this
is a suicide mission it

00:08:28.570 --> 00:08:32.300
is the most expensive and
longest suicide in history.

00:08:32.300 --> 00:08:35.483
It's eight years and all of my
time and energy and passion.

00:08:36.874 --> 00:08:38.853
So this is a very bad
way to kill yourself.

00:08:53.380 --> 00:08:55.610
I think we have now
succeeded in building the

00:08:55.610 --> 00:08:58.600
thousand dollar spacesuit.
Nobody will believe

00:08:58.600 --> 00:09:01.360
me of course until I
test it and prove it in

00:09:01.360 --> 00:09:04.543
space conditions. And it
can work, it can work.

00:09:07.380 --> 00:09:10.180
Because there's no wind I'd
like to kinda go dead center

00:09:11.321 --> 00:09:14.132
of the playa, and then
we'll set everything up.

00:09:14.132 --> 00:09:15.070
And no matter which way
the wind is going we've

00:09:15.070 --> 00:09:19.277
got.. Yeah. OK, any questions?

00:09:21.152 --> 00:09:21.993
OK, great.

00:09:22.890 --> 00:09:23.723
Thanks, guys.

00:09:28.580 --> 00:09:31.503
We've gotta pull the
sides up. Watch your head.

00:09:39.717 --> 00:09:40.550
Standby.

00:09:44.784 --> 00:09:46.510
OK, we can prepare the suit.

00:09:46.510 --> 00:09:47.633
Sun is coming up now.

00:09:55.826 --> 00:09:59.580
OK, it all looks nice and
squared away we should launch.

00:09:59.580 --> 00:10:01.530
We got a fuel quantity check done?

00:10:06.590 --> 00:10:09.730
Just like when I was a kid,
and dad projected these

00:10:09.730 --> 00:10:13.410
Apollo landing films, now
I'm flying a balloon; and

00:10:13.410 --> 00:10:15.730
when I'm wearing this
suit and doing that, well

00:10:15.730 --> 00:10:17.413
that's pretty special for me.

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:20.843
These are childhood dreams coming true.

00:10:21.970 --> 00:10:26.090
Com check. Everything's
good, suit's one PSI.

00:10:26.090 --> 00:10:28.470
I know that there'll be
a moment on the highest

00:10:28.470 --> 00:10:32.130
flight where I'll realize
I've done what I wanted

00:10:32.130 --> 00:10:35.750
to do and everything
works. And it might only

00:10:35.750 --> 00:10:37.020
be 10 seconds in your life.

00:10:37.020 --> 00:10:39.150
Altitude one thousand feet.

00:10:39.150 --> 00:10:42.673
I really believe that when
I get to the very high

00:10:42.673 --> 00:10:44.440
altitude and my suit
is functioning and the

00:10:44.440 --> 00:10:47.382
temperature is right
and my altimeter reads

00:10:47.382 --> 00:10:50.121
63,000 feet. I know that that 10 seconds

00:10:50.121 --> 00:10:53.290
is going to be great, and
it's going to be a treasure

00:10:53.290 --> 00:10:56.710
that I can bring home, and I
can live off that for years

00:10:56.710 --> 00:10:59.900
no matter what calamity comes
along. It doesn't matter,

00:10:59.900 --> 00:11:02.410
I will be able to live
on that 10 seconds of

00:11:02.410 --> 00:11:05.173
experience maybe for the rest of my life.

00:11:08.770 --> 00:11:11.223
That will make everything worth it.

